    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to dig into the biology! The \"goblin\" stuff is just a fun way to visualize it, but the real science is about how your body's systems react to stress. When a client hits a plateau, it's often because their **Central Nervous System (CNS)** is fatigued or their **muscles** are accumulating metabolic waste that needs time to clear out.\n\nHere is the physiological breakdown you can use to explain it to them:\n\n**1. Central Nervous System (CNS) Fatigue**\nWhen you squat heavy, your brain and spinal cord have to fire signals incredibly fast to recruit muscle fibers. If you squat the same weight for three weeks without a break, your CNS becomes slightly fatigued. It's like your brain's \"switchboard\" is getting a little clogged. The deload week isn't just rest; it's a specific period where you reduce the stimulus so the CNS can recover its firing efficiency. Once the CNS is fresh again, the client can recruit more muscle fibers at that same weight, allowing them to move past the plateau.\n\n**2. Metabolic Waste Accumulation**\nDuring heavy squats, muscles produce byproducts like lactate",
      "source": "target",
